  "slug": "exhibit-senescence-like-secretory-5528",
  "summary": "Recent preprints dissect how metaplasia, cAMP signaling and secretory programs govern tissue recovery and fibrosis resolution.",
  "synthesis": "These preprints share a focus on how tissue-resident cells govern recovery, remodeling and secretion rather than on one pathway. The clearest mechanistic thread concerns fibroblasts: endogenous Gs-dependent cAMP production is required for spontaneous resolution of bleomycin-induced lung and skin fibrosis. Quiescent human fibroblasts and preadipocytes display a secretory program (QASP) that overlaps the senescence SASP, varying with induction method and oxygen. In chronic pancreatitis, CD73 deletion in metaplastic epithelial cells worsened fibrosis and immune remodeling, implicating metaplasia in tissue recovery. Separately, MLL4 loss in palatal mesenchyme causes gland hypoplasia linked to reduced Fgf10, SOX9 and Edar, while CSP5, a truncated Cav1 scaffolding peptide, reduces lung injury and mucus expression in mouse models. What is new is that quiescent cells adopt a senescence-like secretory state and that endogenous cAMP and CD73 shape resolution.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
